Preamble
The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for software and other kinds of works, specifically designed to ensure that network-server software remains open and free to its community.

Most licenses for databases and other server software are designed to take away your freedom to share and change it. By contrast, our licenses are intended to guarantee your freedom to share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free software for all its users.

When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not price. Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for them if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you want it, that you can change the software or use pieces of it in new free programs, and that you know you can do these things.

Developers that use our General Public Licenses protect your rights with two steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this License which gives you legal permission to copy, distribute and/or modify the software.

A major limitation of the ordinary GNU General Public License is that it does not cover the use of the software over a network. If someone modifies your software and runs it on a private server, providing access to users over the network, the ordinary GPL does not require them to release their modified source code. 

The GNU Affero General Public License is designed specifically to alter this. Section 13 of this license explicitly requires that if you modify the program and run it on a server to offer network services to users, you MUST make the entire source code of your modified version available to those users at no charge, under the terms of this same license.

